[2026-03-16T03:05:36.210Z] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01J93NhtPQbuA4tbAQYG9242","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# check\n     2→\n     3→## Purpose\n     4→\n     5→Runs typecheck and lint with autofix as a single command. This is the quality gate that must\n     6→pass before every commit.\n     7→\n     8→## Reliability\n     9→\n    10→`npm run check` has a 100% success rate across 173 observed worker iterations (28 invocations,\n    11→all successful). It is the most reliable quality gate and should always be used before commits.\n    12→\n    13→## When to Run\n    14→\n    15→`npm run check` should be the **last command before task completion** — run it as a final\n    16→quality gate, not speculatively mid-task. Running it mid-development wastes cycles on errors\n    17→that will be fixed by subsequent edits. Complete all code changes first, then run check once.\n    18→\n    19→## Usage\n    20→\n    21→- `package.json` entry: `\"check\": \"tsx scripts/check.ts\"`\n    22→- No command-line arguments.\n    23→- No environment variables.\n    24→- **Run from the app directory** (e.g., `cd /repo/apps/AppName && npm run check`), not from the\n    25→  repo root with standalone `npx tsc --noEmit`. Running from the app directory ensures the correct\n    26→  `tsconfig.json` is used and avoids resolution issues.\n    27→- Example: `cd /repo/apps/SalesCRM && npm run check`\n    28→\n    29→## Pre-Flight Disk Space Check\n    30→\n    31→Before running `npm run check`, verify sufficient disk space:\n    32→\n    33→```bash\n    34→df -h / | awk 'NR==2 {print $4}'\n    35→```\n    36→\n    37→If free space is below 1GB, run the disk cleanup procedure in `skills/scripts/disk-cleanup.md`\n    38→before proceeding. TypeScript compilation and ESLint both need temporary disk space — running\n    39→them on a full disk produces confusing errors unrelated to your code.\n    40→\n    41→## Proactive Cleanup Before Running\n    42→\n    43→Before running `npm run check`, proactively clean up unused imports and variables from files\n    44→you just edited. The most common check failure (~80% of lint failures) is `no-unused-vars` /\n    45→`@typescript-eslint/no-unused-vars` from imports or variables left behind during refactoring.\n    46→Scanning your recent edits for stale imports before running the check saves a full\n    47→fail-fix-rerun cycle.\n    48→\n    49→## Incremental Checking for Complex Pages\n    50→\n    51→When building complex pages with 10+ components, run `npm run check` incrementally during\n    52→development (e.g., after every 2-3 components) rather than only at the end. Complex component\n    53→builds commonly produce type errors on the first check, and catching them early prevents\n    54→2-3 fix-and-recheck cycles at the end of the task.\n    55→\n    56→## First-Attempt Failures Are Normal\n    57→\n    58→`npm run check` frequently fails on its first run due to lint errors or type issues introduced\n    59→during development. This is expected behavior, not a blocking problem. When it fails:\n    60→1. Read `logs/check.log` to identify the errors.\n    61→2. Fix the reported issues in your source files **immediately** — do not proceed with other\n    62→   work (writing more components, running tests, etc.) until `npm run check` passes. Lint\n    63→   errors like unused variables accumulate quickly if left unfixed, making later runs harder\n    64→   to diagnose.\n    65→3. Re-run `npm run check`.\n    66→\n    67→## Pre-Existing Lint Failures\n    68→\n    69→If `npm run check` reports lint or type errors in files you did **not** modify, these are\n    70→pre-existing issues. Do not spend multiple attempts trying to fix errors that were not\n    71→introduced by your changes — this wastes significant time. Focus on fixing errors in files\n    72→you touched. If the only failures are in untouched files, you may proceed with your task.\n    73→\n    74→Do not treat a first-attempt failure as a sign that something is fundamentally wrong with the\n    75→check script or project configuration.\n    76→\n    77→## Behavior\n    78→\n    79→1. Run `npx tsc --noEmit` to typecheck. Capture output to a log file.\n    80→2. Run `npx eslint . --fix` to lint with autofix. Capture output to the same log file.\n    81→3. Print a one-line summary to stdout:\n    82→   - Success: `check passed`\n    83→   - Failure: `check failed (typecheck|lint) — see logs/check.log`\n    84→4. Exit with code 0 if both pass, 1 otherwise.\n    85→\n    86→## Inputs\n    87→\n    88→None.\n    89→\n    90→## Outputs\n    91→\n    92→- **stdout**: One-line summary only.\n    93→- **`logs/check.log`**: Full typecheck and lint output. Overwritten each run.\n    94→- **Exit code 0**: both typecheck and lint passed.\n    95→- **Exit code 1**: typecheck or lint failed.\n    96→- Lint autofix may modify source files in place.\n    97→\n    98→## ESLint Configuration\n    99→\n   100→Before running lint, verify that the app has an ESLint configuration file (`.eslintrc.*` or\n   101→`eslint.config.*`). If no configuration exists:\n   102→- Either create a minimal ESLint config for the app before running lint, or\n   103→- Skip the lint step entirely.\n   104→\n   105→Do NOT retry `npx eslint` expecting different results when the underlying issue is a missing\n   106→configuration file. The \"no config found\" error will not resolve on its own.\n   107→\n   108→## TypeScript Checking Retry Limits\n   109→\n   110→Do not retry `npx tsc --noEmit` (or `npm run check`) more than 2–3 times for the same error.\n   111→If it fails repeatedly with the same type errors, the issue is in the code, not a transient\n   112→problem. If it appears to hang (no output for 60+ seconds), check for configuration issues\n   113→in `tsconfig.json` (e.g., incorrect `jsx` flag, missing `include` paths) rather than retrying.\n   114→\n   115→## Faster Type-Only Checks\n   116→\n   117→During iterative development when you only need to verify types (not lint), you can run\n   118→`npx tsc --noEmit` directly from the app directory for faster feedback. This skips the lint\n   119→step and is useful when making rapid type-level changes. Always run the full `npm run check`\n   120→before committing.\n   121→\n   122→## Partial Success (Lint Warnings)\n   123→\n   124→`npm run check` may exit 0 (success) while still producing lint warnings in `logs/check.log`.\n   125→Warnings do not block the build or commit. Only lint **errors** cause a non-zero exit code.\n   126→When reviewing `check.log`, distinguish between warnings (informational, no action required)\n   127→and errors (must be fixed before committing). Common warning-level rules include\n   128→`no-console` and `@typescript-eslint/no-explicit-any`.\n   129→\n   130→## Interpreting Failures\n   131→\n   132→When `npm run check` fails, read `logs/check.log` to determine which step failed and why:\n   133→\n   134→- **Typecheck failures** (`tsc`): Look for `error TS` lines. Common categories:\n   135→  - `TS2307` (Cannot find module): Missing dependency — run `npm install`.\n   136→  - `TS2339` (Property does not exist): Typo in property name or missing type definition.\n   137→  - `TS2345`/`TS2322` (Type mismatch): Wrong type passed to a function or assigned to a variable.\n   138→  - Multiple errors in the same file: Fix the first error — later errors are often cascading.\n   139→- **Lint failures** (`eslint`): Look for rule names in the output (e.g., `no-unused-vars`).\n   140→  Many lint errors are auto-fixed by `--fix`. If errors persist after the run, they require\n   141→  manual fixes. Common error-level rules that need manual fixes:\n   142→  - `no-unused-vars` / `@typescript-eslint/no-unused-vars`: Remove the unused import or variable,\n   143→    or prefix with `_` if it is intentionally unused (e.g., `_event`).\n   144→  - `no-explicit-any`: Replace `any` with a specific type. Use `unknown` if the type is truly\n   145→    unknown and add type narrowing where the value is used.\n   146→  - `prefer-const`: Change `let` to `const` for variables that are never reassigned.\n   147→  - `no-empty`: Add a comment inside empty catch/if blocks (e.g., `// intentionally empty`)\n   148→    or remove the empty block.\n   149→- **Both fail**: Fix typecheck errors first. Lint errors often disappear once types are correct.\n   150→\n   151→During iterative development, typecheck/lint failures are expected. They are part of the\n   152→normal build-fix-check cycle. Focus on fixing the errors rather than treating each failure\n   153→as a problem with the check script itself.\n   154→\n   155→## Troubleshooting Multi-Attempt Failures\n   156→\n   157→`npm run check` has a ~20% multi-attempt rate. Common reasons for needing retries:\n   158→\n   159→- **Cascading type errors**: A single root-cause type error produces many downstream errors.\n   160→  Fix the first `error TS` line and re-run — most other errors often disappear.\n   161→- **Lint autofix conflicts with types**: `eslint --fix` may rewrite code in a way that\n   162→  introduces new type errors. If check fails after lint autofix, re-run — the second pass\n   163→  usually catches both.\n   164→- **Stale build artifacts**: If errors reference files you've already fixed, delete\n   165→  `node_modules/.cache` and re-run.\n   166→\n   167→When `npm run check` fails, always fix and re-run rather than switching to manual\n   168→`npx tsc` / `npx eslint` invocations, which may use different configs.\n   169→\n   170→## Common Lint Error Fix Patterns\n   171→\n   172→These are the most frequent lint errors that require manual fixes after `eslint --fix`:\n   173→\n   174→- **`react-hooks/exhaustive-deps` (setState in useEffect)**: An effect calls a state setter\n   175→  that isn't in the dependency array, or has a dependency that causes infinite re-renders.\n   176→  Fix: move the setter into the dependency array, or restructure to use a ref or callback\n   177→  pattern. Example — change `useEffect(() => { setFoo(bar) }, [bar])` to include `setFoo`\n   178→  in deps (stable from useState) or combine state updates.\n   179→\n   180→- **`react-compiler/react-compiler` (ref access during render)**: Reading `.current` from a\n   181→  ref during render is unsafe because refs are mutable and don't trigger re-renders. Fix:\n   182→  move the ref read into a `useEffect`, event handler, or callback — never in the render\n   183→  body or useMemo/useCallback.\n   184→\n   185→- **`no-unused-vars` / `@typescript-eslint/no-unused-vars`**: Remove the unused import or\n   186→  variable. If intentionally unused (e.g., rest parameter), prefix with `_`. This is the\n   187→  most common lint error (~80% of failures) — proactively clean up imports before running.\n   188→\n   189→## Common Issues\n   190→\n   191→- **`.netlify/functions-serve/` lint errors**: The `.netlify/functions-serve/` directory contains\n   192→  build artifacts that may produce lint errors. These errors are not actionable — do not attempt\n   193→  to fix them. If `npm run check` reports lint failures only in `.netlify/functions-serve/` paths,\n   194→  they can be safely ignored. To prevent these from appearing, add `.netlify` to the ESLint\n   195→  ignore configuration (e.g., `ignorePatterns: ['.netlify']` in `.eslintrc` or an equivalent\n   196→  entry in `eslint.config.*`).\n   197→\n   198→- **`@neondatabase/serverless` resolution errors**: If `tsc` reports module resolution failures\n   199→  for `@neondatabase/serverless`, verify the package is installed: `ls node_modules/@neondatabase/serverless`.\n   200→  If missing, run `npm install` from the app directory. Note that `tsx`-based scripts (e.g.,\n   201→  `npx tsx scripts/schema.ts`) may fail on this dependency even when it is installed — this is a\n   202→  known `tsx` runtime resolution issue. Use `npm run` wrappers instead of direct `npx tsx` invocation.\n   203→\n   204→## Implementation Tips\n   205→\n   206→- Use `child_process.execSync` for each step. Do NOT inherit stdio — pipe output to the log file.\n   207→- Run typecheck first — no point linting if types are broken.\n   208→- Overwrite `logs/check.log` each run (not append).\n   209→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"787afa2e-d7a6-4fb6-9e6b-c697ce29f4b8","uuid":"ceddc3e5-eb47-4c88-892e-ddabae11c2d2","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/repo/skills/scripts/check.md","content":"# check\n\n## Purpose\n\nRuns typecheck and lint with autofix as a single command. This is the quality gate that must\npass before every commit.\n\n## Reliability\n\n`npm run check` has a 100% success rate across 173 observed worker iterations (28 invocations,\nall successful). It is the most reliable quality gate and should always be used before commits.\n\n## When to Run\n\n`npm run check` should be the **last command before task completion** — run it as a final\nquality gate, not speculatively mid-task. Running it mid-development wastes cycles on errors\nthat will be fixed by subsequent edits. Complete all code changes first, then run check once.\n\n## Usage\n\n- `package.json` entry: `\"check\": \"tsx scripts/check.ts\"`\n- No command-line arguments.\n- No environment variables.\n- **Run from the app directory** (e.g., `cd /repo/apps/AppName && npm run check`), not from the\n  repo root with standalone `npx tsc --noEmit`. Running from the app directory ensures the correct\n  `tsconfig.json` is used and avoids resolution issues.\n- Example: `cd /repo/apps/SalesCRM && npm run check`\n\n## Pre-Flight Disk Space Check\n\nBefore running `npm run check`, verify sufficient disk space:\n\n```bash\ndf -h / | awk 'NR==2 {print $4}'\n```\n\nIf free space is below 1GB, run the disk cleanup procedure in `skills/scripts/disk-cleanup.md`\nbefore proceeding. TypeScript compilation and ESLint both need temporary disk space — running\nthem on a full disk produces confusing errors unrelated to your code.\n\n## Proactive Cleanup Before Running\n\nBefore running `npm run check`, proactively clean up unused imports and variables from files\nyou just edited. The most common check failure (~80% of lint failures) is `no-unused-vars` /\n`@typescript-eslint/no-unused-vars` from imports or variables left behind during refactoring.\nScanning your recent edits for stale imports before running the check saves a full\nfail-fix-rerun cycle.\n\n## Incremental Checking for Complex Pages\n\nWhen building complex pages with 10+ components, run `npm run check` incrementally during\ndevelopment (e.g., after every 2-3 components) rather than only at the end. Complex component\nbuilds commonly produce type errors on the first check, and catching them early prevents\n2-3 fix-and-recheck cycles at the end of the task.\n\n## First-Attempt Failures Are Normal\n\n`npm run check` frequently fails on its first run due to lint errors or type issues introduced\nduring development. This is expected behavior, not a blocking problem. When it fails:\n1. Read `logs/check.log` to identify the errors.\n2. Fix the reported issues in your source files **immediately** — do not proceed with other\n   work (writing more components, running tests, etc.) until `npm run check` passes. Lint\n   errors like unused variables accumulate quickly if left unfixed, making later runs harder\n   to diagnose.\n3. Re-run `npm run check`.\n\n## Pre-Existing Lint Failures\n\nIf `npm run check` reports lint or type errors in files you did **not** modify, these are\npre-existing issues. Do not spend multiple attempts trying to fix errors that were not\nintroduced by your changes — this wastes significant time. Focus on fixing errors in files\nyou touched. If the only failures are in untouched files, you may proceed with your task.\n\nDo not treat a first-attempt failure as a sign that something is fundamentally wrong with the\ncheck script or project configuration.\n\n## Behavior\n\n1. Run `npx tsc --noEmit` to typecheck. Capture output to a log file.\n2. Run `npx eslint . --fix` to lint with autofix. Capture output to the same log file.\n3. Print a one-line summary to stdout:\n   - Success: `check passed`\n   - Failure: `check failed (typecheck|lint) — see logs/check.log`\n4. Exit with code 0 if both pass, 1 otherwise.\n\n## Inputs\n\nNone.\n\n## Outputs\n\n- **stdout**: One-line summary only.\n- **`logs/check.log`**: Full typecheck and lint output. Overwritten each run.\n- **Exit code 0**: both typecheck and lint passed.\n- **Exit code 1**: typecheck or lint failed.\n- Lint autofix may modify source files in place.\n\n## ESLint Configuration\n\nBefore running lint, verify that the app has an ESLint configuration file (`.eslintrc.*` or\n`eslint.config.*`). If no configuration exists:\n- Either create a minimal ESLint config for the app before running lint, or\n- Skip the lint step entirely.\n\nDo NOT retry `npx eslint` expecting different results when the underlying issue is a missing\nconfiguration file. The \"no config found\" error will not resolve on its own.\n\n## TypeScript Checking Retry Limits\n\nDo not retry `npx tsc --noEmit` (or `npm run check`) more than 2–3 times for the same error.\nIf it fails repeatedly with the same type errors, the issue is in the code, not a transient\nproblem. If it appears to hang (no output for 60+ seconds), check for configuration issues\nin `tsconfig.json` (e.g., incorrect `jsx` flag, missing `include` paths) rather than retrying.\n\n## Faster Type-Only Checks\n\nDuring iterative development when you only need to verify types (not lint), you can run\n`npx tsc --noEmit` directly from the app directory for faster feedback. This skips the lint\nstep and is useful when making rapid type-level changes. Always run the full `npm run check`\nbefore committing.\n\n## Partial Success (Lint Warnings)\n\n`npm run check` may exit 0 (success) while still producing lint warnings in `logs/check.log`.\nWarnings do not block the build or commit. Only lint **errors** cause a non-zero exit code.\nWhen reviewing `check.log`, distinguish between warnings (informational, no action required)\nand errors (must be fixed before committing). Common warning-level rules include\n`no-console` and `@typescript-eslint/no-explicit-any`.\n\n## Interpreting Failures\n\nWhen `npm run check` fails, read `logs/check.log` to determine which step failed and why:\n\n- **Typecheck failures** (`tsc`): Look for `error TS` lines. Common categories:\n  - `TS2307` (Cannot find module): Missing dependency — run `npm install`.\n  - `TS2339` (Property does not exist): Typo in property name or missing type definition.\n  - `TS2345`/`TS2322` (Type mismatch): Wrong type passed to a function or assigned to a variable.\n  - Multiple errors in the same file: Fix the first error — later errors are often cascading.\n- **Lint failures** (`eslint`): Look for rule names in the output (e.g., `no-unused-vars`).\n  Many lint errors are auto-fixed by `--fix`. If errors persist after the run, they require\n  manual fixes. Common error-level rules that need manual fixes:\n  - `no-unused-vars` / `@typescript-eslint/no-unused-vars`: Remove the unused import or variable,\n    or prefix with `_` if it is intentionally unused (e.g., `_event`).\n  - `no-explicit-any`: Replace `any` with a specific type. Use `unknown` if the type is truly\n    unknown and add type narrowing where the value is used.\n  - `prefer-const`: Change `let` to `const` for variables that are never reassigned.\n  - `no-empty`: Add a comment inside empty catch/if blocks (e.g., `// intentionally empty`)\n    or remove the empty block.\n- **Both fail**: Fix typecheck errors first. Lint errors often disappear once types are correct.\n\nDuring iterative development, typecheck/lint failures are expected. They are part of the\nnormal build-fix-check cycle. Focus on fixing the errors rather than treating each failure\nas a problem with the check script itself.\n\n## Troubleshooting Multi-Attempt Failures\n\n`npm run check` has a ~20% multi-attempt rate. Common reasons for needing retries:\n\n- **Cascading type errors**: A single root-cause type error produces many downstream errors.\n  Fix the first `error TS` line and re-run — most other errors often disappear.\n- **Lint autofix conflicts with types**: `eslint --fix` may rewrite code in a way that\n  introduces new type errors. If check fails after lint autofix, re-run — the second pass\n  usually catches both.\n- **Stale build artifacts**: If errors reference files you've already fixed, delete\n  `node_modules/.cache` and re-run.\n\nWhen `npm run check` fails, always fix and re-run rather than switching to manual\n`npx tsc` / `npx eslint` invocations, which may use different configs.\n\n## Common Lint Error Fix Patterns\n\nThese are the most frequent lint errors that require manual fixes after `eslint --fix`:\n\n- **`react-hooks/exhaustive-deps` (setState in useEffect)**: An effect calls a state setter\n  that isn't in the dependency array, or has a dependency that causes infinite re-renders.\n  Fix: move the setter into the dependency array, or restructure to use a ref or callback\n  pattern. Example — change `useEffect(() => { setFoo(bar) }, [bar])` to include `setFoo`\n  in deps (stable from useState) or combine state updates.\n\n- **`react-compiler/react-compiler` (ref access during render)**: Reading `.current` from a\n  ref during render is unsafe because refs are mutable and don't trigger re-renders. Fix:\n  move the ref read into a `useEffect`, event handler, or callback — never in the render\n  body or useMemo/useCallback.\n\n- **`no-unused-vars` / `@typescript-eslint/no-unused-vars`**: Remove the unused import or\n  variable. If intentionally unused (e.g., rest parameter), prefix with `_`. This is the\n  most common lint error (~80% of failures) — proactively clean up imports before running.\n\n## Common Issues\n\n- **`.netlify/functions-serve/` lint errors**: The `.netlify/functions-serve/` directory contains\n  build artifacts that may produce lint errors. These errors are not actionable — do not attempt\n  to fix them. If `npm run check` reports lint failures only in `.netlify/functions-serve/` paths,\n  they can be safely ignored. To prevent these from appearing, add `.netlify` to the ESLint\n  ignore configuration (e.g., `ignorePatterns: ['.netlify']` in `.eslintrc` or an equivalent\n  entry in `eslint.config.*`).\n\n- **`@neondatabase/serverless` resolution errors**: If `tsc` reports module resolution failures\n  for `@neondatabase/serverless`, verify the package is installed: `ls node_modules/@neondatabase/serverless`.\n  If missing, run `npm install` from the app directory. Note that `tsx`-based scripts (e.g.,\n  `npx tsx scripts/schema.ts`) may fail on this dependency even when it is installed — this is a\n  known `tsx` runtime resolution issue. Use `npm run` wrappers instead of direct `npx tsx` invocation.\n\n## Implementation Tips\n\n- Use `child_process.execSync` for each step. Do NOT inherit stdio — pipe output to the log file.\n- Run typecheck first — no point linting if types are broken.\n- Overwrite `logs/check.log` each run (not append).\n","numLines":209,"startLine":1,"totalLines":209}}}
